DEEP = [
T("Nested JSON from tools and APIs",
  "Security and monitoring tools return JSON with objects inside lists inside objects. You reach the data by chaining keys and indexes, one level at a time. `.get(key, default)` reads a field that might be missing without a KeyError.",
  "import json\n\nraw = '{\"alerts\": [{\"host\": \"web-01\", \"severity\": \"high\"}, {\"host\": \"db-02\"}]}'\ndata = json.loads(raw)\nfirst = data['alerts'][0]\nprint(first['host'])                          # web-01\nprint(data['alerts'][1].get('severity', 'unknown'))   # unknown",
  "`json.loads` builds ordinary dictionaries and lists, so everything you know about indexing and looping applies. Each `[...]` steps one level deeper.",
  "Reading alerts from a SIEM, scan results from a vulnerability scanner, or user lists from an identity API."),
C("High-severity hosts",
  "Parse `raw` and print a list of the hosts whose alerts have severity 'high'. It should print: ['web-01', 'vpn-gw']",
  "import json\n\nraw = '{\"alerts\": [{\"host\": \"web-01\", \"severity\": \"high\"}, {\"host\": \"db-02\", \"severity\": \"low\"}, {\"host\": \"vpn-gw\", \"severity\": \"high\"}]}'\n# TODO: print the hosts of high-severity alerts\n",
  "import json\n\nraw = '{\"alerts\": [{\"host\": \"web-01\", \"severity\": \"high\"}, {\"host\": \"db-02\", \"severity\": \"low\"}, {\"host\": \"vpn-gw\", \"severity\": \"high\"}]}'\ndata = json.loads(raw)\nprint([a['host'] for a in data['alerts'] if a['severity'] == 'high'])",
  "data = json.loads(raw)\nprint([a['host'] for a in data['alerts'] if a['severity'] == 'high'])",
  [("Parse, then step in", "`json.loads(raw)` gives a dictionary. `data['alerts']` is the list of alert dictionaries inside it.",
    "import json\nd = json.loads('{\"items\": [{\"n\": 1}, {\"n\": 2}]}')\nprint(d['items'][1]['n'])  # 2"),
   ("Filter the list", "A comprehension with an `if` keeps only matching alerts: `[a['host'] for a in ... if a['severity'] == 'high']`.",
    "rows = [{'k': 'x', 'v': 1}, {'k': 'y', 'v': 5}]\nprint([r['k'] for r in rows if r['v'] > 2])  # ['y']")]),
T("JSON types and what can't be saved",
  "JSON only has objects, arrays, strings, numbers, true/false and null. Python's dict, list, str, int/float, bool and None map onto them. Anything else, like a set, a date or your own object, makes `json.dumps` raise TypeError. `sort_keys=True` sorts keys so output is stable, and `default=str` turns unknown types into text.",
  "import json\nfrom datetime import date\n\nprint(json.dumps({'ok': True, 'owner': None}))   # {\"ok\": true, \"owner\": null}\nprint(json.dumps({'b': 1, 'a': 2}, sort_keys=True))   # {\"a\": 2, \"b\": 1}\nprint(json.dumps({'seen': date(2025, 10, 1)}, default=str))",
  "`dumps` walks the data and writes each value in JSON's syntax. For a type it doesn't know, it calls the `default` function if you gave one, and otherwise raises TypeError.",
  "Saving reports and sending data to APIs. Stable key order also makes JSON files easy to compare in version control."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["{\"ok\": true, \"owner\": null}", "{'ok': True, 'owner': None}", "{\"ok\": True, \"owner\": None}"], "{\"ok\": true, \"owner\": null}",
  "JSON uses double quotes and writes True and None as `true` and `null`.",
  code="import json\nprint(json.dumps({'ok': True, 'owner': None}))"),
T("Bad JSON: catch the decode error",
  "Logs are often stored as **JSON Lines**: one JSON object per line. A truncated or corrupted line makes `json.loads` raise `json.JSONDecodeError` (a kind of ValueError). Catch it so one bad line doesn't stop the whole run.",
  "import json\n\nfor line in ['{\"user\": \"ada\"}', '{\"user\": ']:\n    try:\n        print(json.loads(line)['user'])\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        print('skipped a bad line')",
  "The parser stops at the first character that breaks JSON's rules and raises an error that says where. A small try block around the parse handles just that failure.",
  "Processing log files, webhooks and exports where you can't trust every record to be complete."),
C("Survive a corrupted log",
  "Parse each line of `lines`, skipping any that aren't valid JSON, and print the users of the 'login' events. It should print: ['ada', 'bob']",
  "import json\n\nlines = [\n    '{\"event\": \"login\", \"user\": \"ada\"}',\n    '{\"event\": \"logout\", \"user\": \"ada\"',\n    '{\"event\": \"login\", \"user\": \"bob\"}',\n    'not json',\n]\nusers = []\n# TODO: parse each line, skip bad ones, collect login users\n\nprint(users)",
  "import json\n\nlines = [\n    '{\"event\": \"login\", \"user\": \"ada\"}',\n    '{\"event\": \"logout\", \"user\": \"ada\"',\n    '{\"event\": \"login\", \"user\": \"bob\"}',\n    'not json',\n]\nusers = []\nfor line in lines:\n    try:\n        record = json.loads(line)\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        continue\n    if record['event'] == 'login':\n        users.append(record['user'])\n\nprint(users)",
  "for line in lines:\n    try:\n        record = json.loads(line)\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        continue\n    if record['event'] == 'login':\n        users.append(record['user'])",
  [("Keep the try small", "Wrap only `json.loads(line)` in try. `continue` in the except jumps to the next line.",
    "for word in ['1', 'x', '3']:\n    try:\n        n = int(word)\n    except ValueError:\n        continue\n    print(n)"),
   ("Then check the event", "After a successful parse, test `record['event'] == 'login'` and append `record['user']`. The second line is cut off and the fourth isn't JSON, so both are skipped.",
    "import json\nr = json.loads('{\"event\": \"login\", \"user\": \"cy\"}')\nprint(r['event'] == 'login', r['user'])")]),
T("datetime: dates with times",
  "`datetime` holds a date and a time. Logs usually store it in ISO 8601 form, like `2025-03-14T09:30:00`, which `datetime.fromisoformat` reads directly (and `date.fromisoformat` reads `2025-03-14`). `.hour`, `.minute` and `.date()` pull out parts.",
  "from datetime import datetime, date\n\nseen = datetime.fromisoformat('2025-03-14T09:30:00')\nprint(seen.hour, seen.minute)     # 9 30\nprint(seen.date())                # 2025-03-14\nprint(date.fromisoformat('2025-12-01').month)   # 12",
  "ISO format sorts correctly as text and has one fixed layout, so it parses without a format string.",
  "Login times, scan times, ticket timestamps: nearly every IT record carries one."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["9", "14", "30"], "9",
  "In ISO format the time follows the T: 09:30:00, so the hour is 9.",
  code="from datetime import datetime\nprint(datetime.fromisoformat('2025-03-14T09:30:00').hour)"),
T("Expiry checks with timedelta",
  "Policies are date math: certificates expire, passwords must change every 90 days, accounts lock after inactivity. Subtract dates to get a `timedelta`, then compare its `.days`, or add `timedelta(days=90)` to find a deadline.",
  "from datetime import date, timedelta\n\nchanged = date(2025, 6, 1)\ntoday = date(2025, 10, 1)\nprint((today - changed).days)            # 122\nprint(today - changed > timedelta(days=90))   # True: password too old\nprint(changed + timedelta(days=90))      # 2025-08-30",
  "Dates know the real calendar, so month lengths and leap years are handled for you. Comparing two timedeltas works like comparing numbers.",
  "Certificate renewal alerts, password-age checks, warranty end dates and stale-account cleanup."),
C("Certificate expiry",
  "Work out how many days are left before the certificate expires, then print the number and 'renew' if it's under 30 days, otherwise 'ok'. It should print: 22 renew",
  "from datetime import date\n\nexpires = date(2025, 11, 15)\ntoday = date(2025, 10, 24)\n# TODO: print days left and 'renew' or 'ok'\n",
  "from datetime import date\n\nexpires = date(2025, 11, 15)\ntoday = date(2025, 10, 24)\ndays_left = (expires - today).days\nprint(days_left, 'renew' if days_left < 30 else 'ok')",
  "days_left = (expires - today).days\nprint(days_left, 'renew' if days_left < 30 else 'ok')",
  [("Later minus earlier", "`expires - today` is a timedelta. Its `.days` is the whole number of days between them.",
    "from datetime import date\nprint((date(2025, 1, 10) - date(2025, 1, 1)).days)  # 9"),
   ("One line decides", "A conditional expression picks the word: `'renew' if days_left < 30 else 'ok'`. Print both values with one print.",
    "n = 40\nprint(n, 'renew' if n < 30 else 'ok')")]),
Q("What does this code print?", ["5 86405.0", "86405 86405.0", "5 5.0"], "5 86405.0",
  "`.seconds` is only the seconds part left after whole days, from 0 to 86399. `.total_seconds()` is the full length. Mixing them up is a common timing bug.",
  code="from datetime import timedelta\nd = timedelta(days=1, seconds=5)\nprint(d.seconds, d.total_seconds())",
  feedback={"86405 86405.0": "`.seconds` leaves out the whole days. Use `.total_seconds()` for the full duration."}),
T("Parsing log timestamps",
  "Many logs don't use ISO format. `datetime.strptime(text, pattern)` parses any layout you describe: `%H` hour (00-23), `%M` minute, `%S` second, plus `%Y-%m-%d` for the date. Once parsed, timestamps sort and subtract correctly.",
  "from datetime import datetime\n\nfmt = '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'\nstart = datetime.strptime('2025-10-01 09:14:00', fmt)\nend = datetime.strptime('2025-10-01 09:20:30', fmt)\nprint(end - start)                       # 0:06:30\nprint((end - start).total_seconds() / 60)   # 6.5",
  "The pattern tells strptime where each number sits in the text. If the text doesn't match, it raises ValueError and says so.",
  "Incident timelines: when the first alert fired, when it was contained, and how long that took."),
C("Incident duration",
  "This incident ran overnight. Parse the first and last timestamps and print how many minutes it lasted in total. It should print: 1548.5",
  "from datetime import datetime\n\nfirst_alert = '2025-10-01 09:14:00'\ncontained = '2025-10-02 11:02:30'\n# TODO: print the duration in minutes\n",
  "from datetime import datetime\n\nfirst_alert = '2025-10-01 09:14:00'\ncontained = '2025-10-02 11:02:30'\nfmt = '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'\nduration = datetime.strptime(contained, fmt) - datetime.strptime(first_alert, fmt)\nprint(duration.total_seconds() / 60)",
  "fmt = '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'\nduration = datetime.strptime(contained, fmt) - datetime.strptime(first_alert, fmt)\nprint(duration.total_seconds() / 60)",
  [("One pattern for both", "Both strings have the layout `'%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'`. Parse each with `datetime.strptime(text, fmt)`.",
    "from datetime import datetime\nprint(datetime.strptime('2025-01-02 03:04:05', '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S').minute)  # 4"),
   ("Seconds, then minutes", "Subtract to get a timedelta, then `.total_seconds() / 60`. `.seconds` would drop the whole day here and give 108.5.",
    "from datetime import timedelta\nprint(timedelta(hours=1, minutes=30).total_seconds() / 60)  # 90.0")]),
T("Unix timestamps and UTC",
  "Many systems log time as a **Unix timestamp**: seconds since 1 January 1970 UTC. `datetime.fromtimestamp(ts, tz=timezone.utc)` turns one into a readable time. Keep log times in UTC: local time shifts with daylight saving and differs between servers, which scrambles timelines.",
  "from datetime import datetime, timezone\n\nts = 1700000000\nwhen = datetime.fromtimestamp(ts, tz=timezone.utc)\nprint(when.isoformat())    # 2023-11-14T22:13:20+00:00",
  "The `tz` argument attaches a time zone, making the datetime \"aware\". `.isoformat()` then includes the offset, `+00:00` for UTC.",
  "Correlating events across servers, cloud logs and APIs, which almost always use UTC."),
Q("Why should servers log times in UTC?", ["One shared clock avoids daylight-saving jumps and time-zone mix-ups when combining logs", "UTC timestamps take less disk space", "Local time can't be parsed"], "One shared clock avoids daylight-saving jumps and time-zone mix-ups when combining logs",
  "Convert to local time only when showing a person. Store and compare in UTC."),
C("Read an epoch timestamp",
  "Convert the Unix timestamp to a UTC datetime and print it in ISO format. It should print: 2023-11-14T22:13:20+00:00",
  "from datetime import datetime, timezone\n\nts = 1700000000\n# TODO: print the UTC time in ISO format\n",
  "from datetime import datetime, timezone\n\nts = 1700000000\nprint(datetime.fromtimestamp(ts, tz=timezone.utc).isoformat())",
  "print(datetime.fromtimestamp(ts, tz=timezone.utc).isoformat())",
  [("Pass the time zone", "`datetime.fromtimestamp(ts, tz=timezone.utc)` reads the seconds as UTC. Without `tz`, Python uses the computer's local zone.",
    "from datetime import datetime, timezone\nprint(datetime.fromtimestamp(0, tz=timezone.utc).year)  # 1970"),
   ("Then format it", "Call `.isoformat()` on the result to get the standard text form with the `+00:00` offset.",
    "from datetime import datetime, timezone\nprint(datetime(2024, 1, 2, tzinfo=timezone.utc).isoformat())")]),
Q("You need to store each host with a list of open ports and nested scan details. Which format fits best?", ["JSON", "CSV", "A plain text file with one value per line"], "JSON",
  "CSV is flat: one row, fixed columns. JSON holds lists and nested objects directly."),
]

INTERVIEW = [
Q("Interview question: what's the difference between `json.load` and `json.loads`?",
  ["`load` reads from an open file; `loads` parses a string", "`loads` is the faster version", "`load` returns a list and `loads` a dictionary"],
  "`load` reads from an open file; `loads` parses a string",
  "The s stands for string. `dump` and `dumps` mirror them for writing."),
Q("`json.dumps({'when': datetime.now()})` raises TypeError. Why, and what's a fix?",
  ["JSON has no date type; convert first, e.g. `.isoformat()` or `default=str`", "datetime objects are always empty", "dumps needs `indent=2` for dates"],
  "JSON has no date type; convert first, e.g. `.isoformat()` or `default=str`",
  "ISO strings are the usual choice because they sort correctly and parse back with `fromisoformat`."),
Q("A password policy says passwords expire after 90 days. Which check is right?", ["`(today - changed).days > 90`", "`today.day - changed.day > 90`", "`today - changed > 90`"], "`(today - changed).days > 90`",
  "Subtracting dates gives a timedelta; `.days` is the gap. `.day` is just the day of the month, and a timedelta can't be compared to a plain int."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["[1, 2]", "(1, 2)", "It raises TypeError"], "[1, 2]",
  "JSON has arrays but no tuples, so a tuple is saved as an array and comes back as a list.",
  code="import json\nprint(json.loads(json.dumps((1, 2))))"),
Q("What does this code print?", ["{'1': 'a'}", "{1: 'a'}", "It raises TypeError"], "{'1': 'a'}",
  "JSON object keys are always strings, so the int key 1 comes back as '1'. A frequent surprise when reloading saved data.",
  code="import json\nprint(json.loads(json.dumps({1: 'a'})))",
  feedback={"{1: 'a'}": "dumps writes the key as \"1\", and loads has no way to know it was an int."}),
C("Classic: inactive account audit",
  "Accounts unused for over 90 days are a risk and should be disabled. Parse `raw` and print the usernames whose last login is more than 90 days before `today`. It should print: ['bob', 'svc-backup']",
  "import json\nfrom datetime import date\n\nraw = '[{\"user\": \"ada\", \"last_login\": \"2025-09-20\"}, {\"user\": \"bob\", \"last_login\": \"2025-05-02\"}, {\"user\": \"svc-backup\", \"last_login\": \"2024-12-31\"}]'\ntoday = date(2025, 10, 1)\n# TODO: print users inactive for more than 90 days\n",
  "import json\nfrom datetime import date\n\nraw = '[{\"user\": \"ada\", \"last_login\": \"2025-09-20\"}, {\"user\": \"bob\", \"last_login\": \"2025-05-02\"}, {\"user\": \"svc-backup\", \"last_login\": \"2024-12-31\"}]'\ntoday = date(2025, 10, 1)\naccounts = json.loads(raw)\nprint([a['user'] for a in accounts if (today - date.fromisoformat(a['last_login'])).days > 90])",
  "accounts = json.loads(raw)\nprint([a['user'] for a in accounts if (today - date.fromisoformat(a['last_login'])).days > 90])",
  [("Turn each text date into a date", "`date.fromisoformat(a['last_login'])` reads '2025-05-02' as a real date you can subtract.",
    "from datetime import date\nprint((date(2025, 10, 1) - date.fromisoformat('2025-09-20')).days)  # 11"),
   ("Filter on the gap", "Keep users where `(today - last).days > 90`. Service accounts like svc-backup are a classic finding in real audits.",
    "gaps = {'a': 11, 'b': 152}\nprint([u for u in gaps if gaps[u] > 90])")]),
C("Classic: brute-force detection",
  "Flag any address with 3 or more failed logins within 5 minutes. Group the timestamps by IP, sort them, and check each run of 3 consecutive failures. Print the flagged addresses sorted. It should print: ['10.0.0.5']",
  "from datetime import datetime, timedelta\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nevents = [\n    ('2025-10-01 09:00:00', '10.0.0.5'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:00:00', '10.0.0.9'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:01:30', '10.0.0.5'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:20:00', '10.0.0.9'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:03:10', '10.0.0.5'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:40:00', '10.0.0.9'),\n    ('2025-10-01 10:00:00', '10.0.0.7'),\n]\n# TODO: flag IPs with 3 failures inside 5 minutes\n",
  "from datetime import datetime, timedelta\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nevents = [\n    ('2025-10-01 09:00:00', '10.0.0.5'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:00:00', '10.0.0.9'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:01:30', '10.0.0.5'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:20:00', '10.0.0.9'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:03:10', '10.0.0.5'),\n    ('2025-10-01 09:40:00', '10.0.0.9'),\n    ('2025-10-01 10:00:00', '10.0.0.7'),\n]\ntimes = defaultdict(list)\nfor stamp, ip in events:\n    times[ip].append(datetime.strptime(stamp, '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'))\nflagged = []\nfor ip, ts in times.items():\n    ts.sort()\n    for i in range(len(ts) - 2):\n        if ts[i + 2] - ts[i] <= timedelta(minutes=5):\n            flagged.append(ip)\n            break\nprint(sorted(flagged))",
  "times = defaultdict(list)\nfor stamp, ip in events:\n    times[ip].append(datetime.strptime(stamp, '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'))\nflagged = []\nfor ip, ts in times.items():\n    ts.sort()\n    for i in range(len(ts) - 2):\n        if ts[i + 2] - ts[i] <= timedelta(minutes=5):\n            flagged.append(ip)\n            break\nprint(sorted(flagged))",
  [("Group, then sort", "Collect each IP's parsed times in `defaultdict(list)`, then sort each list so nearby failures sit next to each other.",
    "from collections import defaultdict\ng = defaultdict(list)\nfor t, k in [(3, 'a'), (1, 'a'), (2, 'b')]:\n    g[k].append(t)\nprint({k: sorted(v) for k, v in g.items()})"),
   ("A window of three", "For sorted times, three failures fit in 5 minutes when `ts[i + 2] - ts[i] <= timedelta(minutes=5)` for some i. Check every i up to `len(ts) - 3`. Sorting makes this O(n log n) per address.",
    "ts = [0, 90, 190, 900]\nprint(any([ts[i + 2] - ts[i] <= 300 for i in range(len(ts) - 2)]))  # True")]),
C("Classic: merge config files",
  "A hardened SSH config starts from defaults and applies overrides. Parse both JSON strings, apply the overrides on top of the defaults with `.update()`, and print the result with `json.dumps(..., sort_keys=True)`. It should print: {\"max_tries\": 3, \"password_auth\": false, \"port\": 22}",
  "import json\n\ndefaults = '{\"port\": 22, \"password_auth\": true, \"max_tries\": 5}'\noverrides = '{\"password_auth\": false, \"max_tries\": 3}'\n# TODO: merge and print\n",
  "import json\n\ndefaults = '{\"port\": 22, \"password_auth\": true, \"max_tries\": 5}'\noverrides = '{\"password_auth\": false, \"max_tries\": 3}'\nconfig = json.loads(defaults)\nconfig.update(json.loads(overrides))\nprint(json.dumps(config, sort_keys=True))",
  "config = json.loads(defaults)\nconfig.update(json.loads(overrides))\nprint(json.dumps(config, sort_keys=True))",
  [("update overwrites matching keys", "`a.update(b)` copies every key of b into a, replacing values that were already there and keeping the rest.",
    "a = {'x': 1, 'y': 2}\na.update({'y': 9})\nprint(a)  # {'x': 1, 'y': 9}"),
   ("Sorted output", "`json.dumps(config, sort_keys=True)` writes keys alphabetically, so the result is the same however the dict was built.",
    "import json\nprint(json.dumps({'b': 1, 'a': 2}, sort_keys=True))")]),
C("Debug: the timestamp won't parse",
  "This raises ValueError: the time data doesn't match the format. The pattern has the day and month swapped. Fix the pattern so it prints: July 31",
  "from datetime import datetime\n\nstamp = '2025-07-31 14:05:00'\nwhen = datetime.strptime(stamp, '%Y-%d-%m %H:%M:%S')\nprint(when.strftime('%B %d'))",
  "from datetime import datetime\n\nstamp = '2025-07-31 14:05:00'\nwhen = datetime.strptime(stamp, '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')\nprint(when.strftime('%B %d'))",
  "The month comes before the day in this log:\n'%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'",
  [("Read the text, then the pattern", "In '2025-07-31', 07 is the month and 31 the day. The pattern says `%d` first, so Python tries to read 31 as a month and fails.",
    "from datetime import datetime\nprint(datetime.strptime('2025-02-03', '%Y-%m-%d').month)  # 2"),
   ("Swap two codes", "Change `%Y-%d-%m` to `%Y-%m-%d`. The time part `%H:%M:%S` is already right.",
    "from datetime import datetime\nprint(datetime.strptime('10:30', '%H:%M').hour)")]),
]
